1
00:00:00,302 --> 00:00:04,406
Suppose your agent needs the acceptance
criteria from a training issue.

2
00:00:04,779 --> 00:00:06,836
How does that issue get into the
session?

3
00:00:07,583 --> 00:00:11,446
Follow the arrows. The client connects
to an M C P server,

4
00:00:11,699 --> 00:00:14,461
and the server offers one read-only
tool.

5
00:00:14,978 --> 00:00:19,504
When the tool is called, it reads a
synthetic issue fixture and sends a

6
00:00:19,504 --> 00:00:20,235
result back.

7
00:00:20,841 --> 00:00:22,892
Let's check the contract before calling
it.

8
00:00:24,241 --> 00:00:28,847
Open the approved integration record. It
names a server, training issues,

9
00:00:29,078 --> 00:00:31,133
and a tool, get training issue.

10
00:00:31,628 --> 00:00:35,700
The tool takes a number and can read
orders issues from the fixture.

11
00:00:36,008 --> 00:00:38,636
Look at the operation and the data scope
together.

12
00:00:39,130 --> 00:00:42,598
If the workspace owner has provided the
lab as a recording,

13
00:00:42,874 --> 00:00:46,869
the same record tells you which action
the captured result represents.

14
00:00:48,635 --> 00:00:51,092
Next, inspect how the approved client
connects.

15
00:00:51,488 --> 00:00:55,766
The real screen depends on whether
you're in Claude Code or OpenCode and on

16
00:00:55,766 --> 00:00:56,741
the installed build.

17
00:00:57,181 --> 00:01:01,899
Find the configured server identity and
scope where the workspace exposes them.

18
00:01:02,438 --> 00:01:07,063
The values on this screen are fixtures.
Use the approved connection as

19
00:01:07,063 --> 00:01:10,964
configured, and keep the lab on this
synthetic data boundary.

20
00:01:12,679 --> 00:01:15,838
Ask for issue seventeen and its
acceptance criteria.

21
00:01:16,267 --> 00:01:20,393
In this example, the client proposes get
training issue with issue number

22
00:01:20,393 --> 00:01:21,148
seventeen.

23
00:01:21,455 --> 00:01:25,978
Check the operation and the number. They
should match the request you made,

24
00:01:26,385 --> 00:01:28,883
and the tool should be approved for that
input.

25
00:01:29,399 --> 00:01:33,646
This is the evidence you want before
responding to any confirmation prompt

26
00:01:33,646 --> 00:01:35,278
shown by your workspace.

27
00:01:37,008 --> 00:01:40,941
The tool returns two requirements.
Positive integers are accepted.

28
00:01:41,336 --> 00:01:46,090
Zero and negative integers are rejected.
Now look at the agent's summary and

29
00:01:46,090 --> 00:01:47,460
check that it carries both.

30
00:01:47,899 --> 00:01:52,090
If it only says to reject zero, the
negative case was lost in the summary.

31
00:01:52,441 --> 00:01:56,889
Correct the summary against the actual
issue text so the next stage can use

32
00:01:56,889 --> 00:01:58,456
both acceptance criteria.

33
00:02:00,219 --> 00:02:04,340
Imagine the next proposed action is to
post the summary to a pull request.

34
00:02:04,757 --> 00:02:09,122
Catch that change in scope. Reading
issue seventeen and writing a comment

35
00:02:09,266 --> 00:02:10,552
are different operations.

36
00:02:10,970 --> 00:02:13,496
For this lab, save the summary in the
session.

37
00:02:13,848 --> 00:02:18,427
In the actual review workflow, the
authorized author or reviewer decides

38
00:02:18,427 --> 00:02:22,303
what to post using their own account and
GitHub CLI.

39
00:02:24,094 --> 00:02:28,328
Finish with a small record: the approved
server, the tool and number,

40
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:30,946
and the two criteria you verified.

41
00:02:31,255 --> 00:02:35,138
A teammate should see exactly what was
read and what matched the source.

42
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:38,821
That is the simplest useful M C P
workflow:

43
00:02:39,316 --> 00:02:42,801
one authorized tool call that brings
evidence into the task,

44
00:02:43,186 --> 00:02:45,329
with a boundary that you can explain.
